GIF: a packet sent to be drained at once goes to the GS without a copy when nothing is queued before it. GifArbiter::submitAndDrain processes it straight from the caller's buffer and then drains anything submitted meanwhile, which is the order a lone queued packet gets; with packets already queued it submits and drains as before. PS2Memory::submitGifPacket uses it when it drains immediately. Checked in Metropolismania: all 4 MB of VRAM byte-identical at the eleven views of a scripted city camera run with frame skip off; PS2Recomp's tests give the same results.

diff --git a/ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/gs/ps2_gif_arbiter.h b/ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/gs/ps2_gif_arbiter.h
--- a/ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/gs/ps2_gif_arbiter.h
+++ b/ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/gs/ps2_gif_arbiter.h
@@ -31,6 +31,8 @@ public:
     void setProcessPacketFn(ProcessPacketFn fn) { m_processFn = std::move(fn); }
 
     void submit(GifPathId pathId, const uint8_t *data, uint32_t sizeBytes, bool path2DirectHl = false);
+    // submit, then drain: the packet is handed over from `data` without a copy when nothing is queued before it.
+    void submitAndDrain(GifPathId pathId, const uint8_t *data, uint32_t sizeBytes, bool path2DirectHl = false);
 
     void drain();
     bool empty() const { return m_queue.empty(); }
diff --git a/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/gs/ps2_gif_arbiter.cpp b/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/gs/ps2_gif_arbiter.cpp
